= Oracle (blockchain) =

== Overview ==
'''Oracle (blockchain)''' is a mechanism or service that supplies data or results from outside a blockchain's native state to smart contracts or other on-chain logic.

Blockchains and smart contracts cannot independently observe arbitrary external facts. Oracles bridge that boundary by importing prices, events, sensor readings, randomness, computation results, or other data.

An oracle does not magically turn external data into truth. Its security depends on sources, aggregation, incentives, signatures, update rules, latency, governance, and the ability to detect or withstand manipulation and outages.

Some oracle systems are centralized; others aggregate multiple reporters, use cryptographic attestations, stake/slashing, trusted hardware, cross-validation, or optimistic dispute mechanisms.

== Boundaries ==
* oracle ≠ truth source by definition
* decentralized oracle ≠ assumption-free oracle
* oracle data ≠ native blockchain state

